WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.374 --> 00:00:02.790
YUFENG GUO: Every data scientist
has different preferences

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:05.110
when it comes to their
programming environment--

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:09.560
vim versus emacs, tabs
versus spaces, Virtualenv

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:11.100
versus Anaconda.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.990
Today I want to share
with you my environment

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:15.520
for working with data and
doing machine learning.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:17.670
You most definitely do
not need to copy my setup,

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:20.790
but perhaps some bits of it
can serve as useful inspiration

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:23.550
for your development
environment.

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:26.530
To start with, we need
to talk about Pip.

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:29.230
Pip is Python's package manager.

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:31.570
It has come built into
Python for quite a while now,

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:35.440
so if you have Python, you
likely have Pip already.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.500
Pip installs packages
like tensorflow and numpy,

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.870
pandas and Jupyter,
and many, many more,

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:42.940
along with their dependencies.

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:45.280
Many Python resources
are delivered

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.800
in some form of Pip packages.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.290
Sometimes you may see a
file called requirements.txt

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:54.160
in someone's folder
of Python scripts.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.670
Typically, that file outlines
all of the Pip packages

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.230
that that project
uses, so you can easily

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:02.275
install everything
needed by using pip

00:01:02.275 --> 00:01:06.430
install -r requirements.txt.

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:08.260
As part of this
ecosystem, there's

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:11.230
a whole world of version
numbers and dependencies.

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:13.180
I sometimes need to
use different versions

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:16.460
of a given library for different
projects that I'm working on.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:19.660
So I need a way to organize
my groups of packages

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:23.300
into different
isolated environments.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:25.540
There are two popular
options currently

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:29.440
for taking care of managing
your different Pip packages--

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:33.450
virtualenv and anaconda.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:35.340
Virtualenv is a
package that allows

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:38.730
you to create named
virtual environments where

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:41.650
you can install Pip packages
in an isolated manner.

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:43.410
This tool is great
if you want to have

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:45.180
detailed control
over which packages

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.940
you install for each
environment you create.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:50.910
For example, you could create an
environment for web development

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.550
with one set of libraries,
and a different environment

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:54.930
for data science.

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:58.260
This way, you won't need to have
unrelated libraries interacting

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:01.110
with each other, and it allows
you to create environments

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:04.320
dedicated to specific purposes.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.560
Now, if you're primarily
doing data science work,

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.910
Anaconda is also a great option.

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:12.810
Anaconda is created by
Continuum Analytics,

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:14.760
and it is a Python
distribution that

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.730
comes preinstalled with lots
of useful Python libraries

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:19.310
for data science.

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:21.300
Anaconda is popular
because it brings

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:23.610
many of the tools used in
data science and machine

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:26.250
learning with just
one install, so it's

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:29.070
great for having a
short and simple setup.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:32.040
Like Virtualenv, Anaconda
also uses the concept

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:35.130
of creating environments so as
to isolate different libraries

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:36.180
and versions.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.960
Anaconda also introduces its
own package manager called conda

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:42.060
from where you can
install libraries.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:45.630
Additionally, Anaconda still has
the useful interaction with Pip

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:48.180
that allows you to install
any additional libraries which

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.540
are not available in the
Anaconda package manager.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:56.910
So-- which one do I use,
virtualenv or anaconda?

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:58.860
Well, I often find
myself testing out

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.680
new versions of tensorflow
and other libraries

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.510
across both Python
2 and Python 3.

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:07.470
So ideally, I would like
to be able to try out

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:10.620
different libraries on both
virtualenv and anaconda,

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:12.690
but sometimes those
two package managers

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:16.560
don't necessarily play nicely
with each other on one system.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.820
So I have opted to use both,
but I manage the whole thing

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:23.760
using a library called pyenv.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:28.080
Conceptually, pyenv sits atop
both virtualenv and Anaconda

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.470
and it can be used to control
not only which virtualenv

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.900
environment or Anaconda
environment is in use,

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:36.060
but it also easily
controls whether I'm

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:39.090
running Python 2 or Python 3.

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:41.970
One final aspect of
pyenv that I enjoy

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:45.180
is the ability to set a
default environment for a given

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:46.410
directory.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:48.270
This causes that
desired environment

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:51.840
to be automatically activated
when I enter a directory.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.210
I find this to be way
easier than trying

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:56.310
to remember which
environment I want to use

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:58.960
every time I work on a project.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:02.190
So which package
manager do you use?

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:06.160
It really comes down to your
workflow and preferences.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:09.150
If you typically just use
the core data science tools

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:12.090
and are not concerned with
having some extra libraries

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.130
installed that you
don't use, Anaconda

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:17.130
can be a great choice, since
it leads to a simpler workflow

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:18.959
for your needs and preferences.

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:22.350
But if you are someone who loves
to customize your environment

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:24.930
and make it exactly
like how you want it,

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:27.780
then perhaps something like
virtualenv or even pyenv

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:29.620
maybe more to your liking.

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:33.210
There's no one right way
to manage Python libraries,

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:35.520
and there's certainly more
out there than the options

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:37.200
that I just presented.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.270
As different tools
come and go, it's

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.610
important to remember that
everyone has different needs

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:42.850
and preferences.

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:46.921
So choose for yourself-- what
tools out there serve you best?

00:04:46.921 --> 00:04:48.420
So what does your
Python moment look

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:51.540
like, and how do you keep it
from getting out of control?

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:55.090
Share your setup in
the comments below.

